# Line endings, decided here rather than by whichever machine checked the repo
# out.
#
# THIS WAS FOUND BY BUILDING ON WINDOWS. git's default on Windows is to convert
# every text file to CRLF on checkout, and sixteen tests failed on the first
# Windows run — none of them about the app. The suite reads its own source and
# asserts on it, so patterns like /- from: vendor\n\s+to: vendor/ simply stop
# matching, and the generated-file check compares text built with \n against a
# file on disk that now has \r\n and reports every one of them stale.
#
# LF everywhere also matters outside the tests: helpers/fractal-remote.sh is a
# shell script, and a shell script with CRLF fails on macOS and Linux with
# "bad interpreter: /usr/bin/env bash^M" — a message that tells you nothing
# about line endings.
#
# `text=auto` still detects binaries and leaves them alone; the PNGs below are
# named anyway, because being wrong about one of those corrupts it silently.
* text=auto eol=lf

*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.ico binary
*.icns binary
*.p12 binary
*.hbc binary

# Windows does not record an executable bit, so git is the only place it is
# kept. The helper is meant to be run, not sourced.
helpers/fractal-remote.sh text eol=lf
